Hackers used some of the world's most popular websites to distribute malware designed to steal cryptocurrency and exploit vulnerabilities in iPhones and iPads this week, according to a new security investigation.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2023/05/bug-security-vulnerability-issue-fix-larry.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-896108" /><br/>
<em><a href="https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/popular-websites-subverted-bid-steal-crypto-target-iphone-users-2026-11-17/">Reuters</a></em> reports that websites associated with 178 organizations were affected by the attack on Monday, November 16, including the likes of Zoom, BBC, CNN, HSBC, Salesforce, and IKEA.<br/>
<br/>
A vulnerability was reportedly exploited in <a href="https://www.optimizely.com">Optimizely</a>, a third-party platform that companies use to test and customize their websites. Hackers managed to insert malicious JavaScript into Optimizely's customer projects, allowing them to distribute the malware through legitimate websites without directly compromising their infrastructure.<br/>
<br/>
Security researcher Scott Helme <a href="https://blog.report-uri.com/worlds-largest-brands-infected-with-malware-via-optimizely/">discovered</a> the attack. Helme found that the malicious scripts could replace legitimate cryptocurrency wallet addresses with ones controlled by the attackers, as well as intercept payment card details, steal browser cookies, and take screenshots.<br/>
<br/>
The code was especially targeted at iPhone and iPad users. Visitors could be served one of two exploit kits – Coruna and DarkSword – through a hidden webpage element, depending on their device's software version. Coruna reportedly targeted devices running iOS 13 through iOS 17, while DarkSword targeted iOS 18. Researchers also found code targeting iOS 26, but this appeared to be limited to identifying devices' characteristics.<br/>
<br/>
The attack potentially allowed malicious code to execute simply by visiting an affected website – users did not need to download an app or interact with a suspicious link. That said, it's still unclear whether any iPhones or iPads were successfully compromised.<br/>
<br/>
Optimizely told <em><a href="https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/optimizely-supply-chain-attack-delivers-malware-to-cnn-fox-news-bbc-salesforce-sites/">BleepingComputer</a></em> that attackers managed to exploit a vulnerability in its account signup process, causing websites using the platform to load malware. The flaw has since been patched, the malicious code has been removed, and affected customers have been notified, according to the company.<br/>
<br/>
